                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I'm torn about whether to recommend this game or not. If we had a rating scale, I would give it a 3/5. Let me explain.\n\nQuantum Flux, at first glance, is a space adventure inspired by games like FTL, and perhaps even The Oregon Trail. It's fun, each time you jump, not knowing what you will find. This is fun, and I find myself excited when the ship launches into a new area.\n\nHowever, after playing a couple of times, you quickly notice that although each jump leads you to a semi-random encounter, the scenario and results of each encounter are always the same. It becomes a memory game rather than a risky adventure. \n\nAdditionally, in the few games I played, the game usually leans towards the positive, so if you choose the option that sounds like the nicest guy would choose, you will get the best results. I kept hoping that this would sometimes backfire, but it was always the same.\n\nThen on my third play, I encountered a bug(?) which is why I will now be uninstalling the game. The whole premise is that you are taking the last of humanity to Haven. Well, I managed to get the ship back into known space, and my crew told me we could now jump straight to Haven. After the conversation, the ship auto-jumped, and I expected an end game screen before credits rolled... but instead I got another random encounter, and at the end of that encounter, it was mentioned that I still had a long way to go. So apparently, it's not possible to actually complete the game.\n\nI should also mention that you cannot adjust the resolution, and it plays in either a tiny window, or a pixelated full screen mode. \n\nTherefore, although there is fun to be had here, and a puzzle to solve in figuring out the best approach to every encounter, the game is in need of some improvements before I could recomend it. If you get it cheap in a bundle, give it a try, otherwise I recommend giving FTL another play instead.",
